#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e2e343-4403-51b2-965f-168bbe606112" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Combination of Horizontal and Vertical Translations"}] :question/id #uuid "9c2447fb-d21a-553f-bc38-243ef63696ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-1445" :question/problem "Identify the equation of the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/36/36a2a45f6078cdf04b798c39b6382c1f40411375a3f16ef77e996b39aa0009b3.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$.\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2$. The parabola is also translated $1$ unit up, so we have $k=1$. We can also see that the vertex is at $(-2,1)$, so again we must have $h=-2$ and $k=1$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the graph is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-2))^{2} + 1 \\\\\ny &= (x + 2)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd6345-30ba-5ecf-8123-8e8d624e794f" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Vertical Translation of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "8106f0ee-a91b-5535-bc05-fe874dc661f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1793" :question/problem "Identify the equation that generated the graph below.\n\n![](images/90/9051b7b34f3b88cbc9b08e0be2ae36b955a081f9cb81a85f667aa6076e3e1859.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(0,k)$.\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units upward. The vertex is located at $(0,3)$.\n\nTherefore, $k=3$, and the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 +3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac1b2aa5-e238-55c5-a515-7b688bc5a960" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Horizontal Translation of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "a301e57f-0f6f-5078-a2fb-7c39fef91f3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1794" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of the function $y=(x+3)^2$. What are the coordinates of the minimum point?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0)$.\n\nIn the given function\n\n$$\ny=(x+3)^2\n$$\n\nwe have $h=-3$. To see this clearly, we can write the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x + 3)^{2} \\\\\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units *left*.\n\n![](images/bf/bffdacd979eaa77a7a64364840ab318bcc4a1c39ca78137146e82f37540e02e4.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-3,0)$, and these are the coordinates of the minimum point."}]}
